# L §4.3: "`at e` is evaluated at the law date the def's own reads need ...; the reads inside e are made at the
# law date of the read that asks for the def (its reader's, or the ask's `law`), never at e's own value, so
# `at day_of(m, surprise_dom)` reads `surprise_dom` once, as its reader would."
# dom is 10 under the 2026 law and 20 from 2027 (a replace).  base is 100.00 PLN until 2027-03-14 and
# 200.00 PLN from 2027-03-15.  r reads pay[2027-03] at reader.  Under law 2026-12-31 dom reads 10, so pay's law
# date is 2027-03-10 and base is 100.00 PLN; under law 2027-06-30 dom reads 20, the law date is 2027-03-20 and
# base is 200.00 PLN.
# held (L §8.2): the open-ended row of dom (v2) is read for 2027-06-30, after the cut 2027-01-01; the base row
# from 2027-03-15 (v3) is read for 2027-03-20, after the cut.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2027-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2027-12-31

source s "s"
unit PLN: currency minor 0.01
input dom: int
input base: money PLN
def pay[m in months]: money PLN = base at day_of(m, dom)
def r[m in months]: money PLN = pay[m] at reader

version v1
  cite s
  governs 2026-01-01..2026-12-31
  known 2025-12-01
  dom = 10

version v2
  cite s
  governs 2027-01-01..
  known 2026-12-01
  dom = 20

version v3
  cite s
  known 2025-12-01
  base = 100.00 governs 2026-01-01..2027-03-14
  base = 200.00 governs 2027-03-15..

ask q1: r[2027-03] law 2026-12-31
ask q2: r[2027-03] law 2027-06-30
=== expect
q1: 100.00 PLN
  law date 2026-12-31, cut 2027-01-01
q2: 200.00 PLN
  held: base = 200.00 PLN (v3), read for 2027-03-20
  held: dom = 20 (v2), read for 2027-06-30
  law date 2027-06-30, cut 2027-01-01
